ending  region  of the unknown which can be critical for  seeking out  family members and for submitting case  reports with dna profile results  sg migrants as i said are found in  criminal databases  so as i've already said marginalization  begets marginalization  through that concept that forced  migrants do not disappear but they are  forcibly disappeared  these structural factors prevent knowing  about the unknown  in the absence of family data accessing  information  on origins by other means is therefore  critical  so we have to figure out where to send  this information  or who to start asking to go ask  around for family members but we can't  do that if we don't know  the geographic location where this  unknown individual originated  so then how can we improve case  identification success  while motivated by these increasing  number of unsuc successful  identifications of missing migrants  how can we address this barrier to  improve rates of positive identification  and repatriation of the border debt so  my response to this question is to  engage try hybrid ancestry estimates  so the proportions of  ancestry for each individual the  case recovery location so the gps  coordinates where that individual was  recovered along the border  and because these um the cases that i'm  working on to build these  this analysis or model is um are already  positively identified  then i have their place of origin or  their sending region already  and i'm using these three sets of  variables to develop  a biogeographic profile  which is a model that allows me to  predict  sending region to aid in the skin  matching  and to enable repatriation  and how is this possible so  genetic diversity in latin america is  very high  studies using genetic and skeletal data  alike have revealed  differences in ancestry for many of the  geospatially defined groups that are  distributed across latin america  so we can use this spatial organization  or structure in ancestry  estimates to reveal information on their  spending regions  we can also capitalize upon information  that we can get from those who are  successful at crossing the border  so we can understand the habits of the  living to shed greater light  on the context of the dead so  demographic research and migration  networks  have found distinctive transit pathways  whereby migrants of different origins  so with different state or city of birth  or residents are associated with  particular destinations  so you come from a particular place you  are more likely to cross the border  at a particular location the pathways  through mexico and the crossing points  along the u.s mexico border  are shared by experience so  if you have family who have successfully  crossed or friends they transmit that  information back to your hometown that  information then  informs the next um persons who  attempt the journey so it's a  perpetualization of these  same transit pathways that are clearly  linked by  point of origin and point of destination  and we can use these trends then to  predict the sending region  for the missing migrants  how did i develop this procedure  so i started out with 150 cases from the  pima county office of the medical  examiner in arizona  i applied model-based clustering  geospatial  analysis machine learning and  traditional statistical methods  to produce this biogeographic profile  and i'll explain how i've done that in  more detail  as we move along to this goal then the  relationships among ancestry composition  so that tri-hybrid  ancestry um breakdown the geographic  location of the remains  so those gps coordinates on the border  the sending region of the place of  origin of the individual  their sex and their case reporting date  were all investigated  and i took that information and built  models that predict the individual's  documented sending region as a micro  regional category  on the one hand and then remarkably it's  even possible to predict the gis  coordinates  for that sending region when those  coordinates are taken as the centroid  of the um area um  state typically from which they  originated so  how do we calculate  craniometric measures of ancestry um so  i'm sure you're familiar with ancestry  estimates being derived from genetic  data  here what i've done is i've rewritten  the mathematics for  the genetic which is categorical  trait analysis to a continuous trait  analysis  so that i produce very similar  results to the genetic data  using almost identical or highly  comparable  mathematical procedures how is this  possible through craniometrics  well measurements of the cranium or  craniometrics have been used as proxies  for genetic data  to study microevolutionary processes  trace patterns of variation  and reveal information on population  history  and individual biology all through  anthropology  for its history and this is because  craniometrics have low plasticity  with high to moderate heritabilities  proportions of ancestry then can be  calculated from cranium metrics  using model-based clustering  implemented with the methods of finite  mixture analysis  and what's exciting about this approach  is that it's an admixture approach  that's comparable to the methods that  are used in genetics  so if you're familiar with the software  admixture or structure  it's the same kind of philosophy and  it's preferable  i would argue to the traditional single  group hard classification methods that  are used  um ubiquitously in forensic anthropology  because  they don't allow us to get at the nuance  of the  geography so by producing these  proportions  we can identify signatures that  represent  geographic regions these geographic  regions need not be  labeled in the same way as a  geopolitical region so we can actually  map them onto real geographic  space and see how they vary and then  create our own  partitions or organization to those that  make  sense based on the  relationships that we're seeing in the  ancestry estimates and  in the gis coordinates  so how is how are these ancestry  estimates actually  produced each individual's ancestry is  reframed as a mixture of parental  contributors  and if it's tri-hybrid we presume that  latin american peoples are  have their ancestry made up by european  african and indigenous components  so we calculate the mixture of those  components for each individual  or we can think of them as the degree of  affinity  between groups as well so once you have  those three  components then you can look at how much  sharing  occurs between different populations  based on the similarity in their  ancestry signatures  and this differs fundamentally from a  single ancestry classification for  multiple reasons first that we start out  with the unknowns  individual and we have a reference  sample of individuals as well  but we strip all of those individuals  from  there we strip away from those  individuals their identifiers  so no sex no population no information  on them whatsoever  we just use their data we subject it to  the model based clustering  and we get a probabilistic allocation so  we can see  how individuals share between all of the  clusters  and each one of those clusters adds up  to one so  sorry of the combination of the clusters  add up to one each one of the clusters  contributes  in some proportion of that one  or 100 percent so individuals vary  in each of those three ancestry  proportions which then serves as  providing a signature  for the individual and then when  aggregated in terms of a population  or a region or however you want to  partition it you get a mean signature  that then allows you for tests of  differences etc  a single ancestry classification starts  out with  predefined groups so presumably  geographically defined populations for  which you have  information you have your unknown  individual  and your unknown individual is allocated  into one of those groups  even though that individual may not  actually be a member of  one of those groups if their true  membership is not represented in the  reference samples  similarly it doesn't address issues of  admixture  which um in current  uh modern times is a really gene  admixture is gene flow  so it's a it's an important factor to  build into our models  so what do i find then after i've done  this analysis where i've looked at the  correspondence between ancestry  proportions  their place of origin their place  of recovery and they these other factors  of sex  and um and cashier well i found most  predominantly that ancestry is  structured by geography  geographic signatures are revealed in  the ancestry proportions as  i had hoped these patterns can be used  to determine than the likely sending  region  so if we see in the um  in the map of mexico we can see that  there is a gradation  or a climb in the degree of european to  indigenous ancestry  and that plays out well when we actually  map these signatures in terms of the  different regions of mexico  so the southeastern part of mexico has  indeed  individuals with the with the signature  whose largest proportion is um  indigenous ancestry  whereas northern mexico the individuals  have  their highest proportion of european  ancestry  and then we can map out how everyone in  between those  two points fall similarly we can  produce the same kind of analysis for  countries  other than mexico so we can look at this  is  um the second part you'll see a series  of countries  and the same tri-hybrid ancestry  proportions that create  a signature that represents guatemala or  a signature that represents  puerto rico and instead of then lumping  all cases as hispanic as is  a tendency in forensic anthropology we  are now  able to get at these individual  populational trends  that allow us to link population with  geography  i also found that the there are  significant spatial relationships  so if we map the cases um  by their region of origin and their  ancestry  along the border um their place of  recovery along the border  it's remarkable to see that the  regions in mexico cluster  in particular places along the border as  i had hoped as well  so recall when i mentioned that we can  learn  about the patterns of the dead by  studying the  transit pathways and migration habits of  the living  this i think exemplifies that  a way of thinking so if you look at the  lower right hand corner map with the  big circles the circle in the center  represents all the migrants from  southern mexico  and then the circles to the right-hand  side  and left-hand side represent um  the traditional area of mexico  and northern mexico and then the central  area  um is falling as an overlap between  the south and the traditional area  so we can see that there are clear  patterns to  where the migrants are coming from and  where they're crossing  and then also we see that there are  patterns in their ancestry  in those um in the positions as well  and um this uh  spatial autocorrelation analysis is a  formal test for that  so it demonstrates that in their  ancestry patterns  there is more evidence of clustering  than if there if it was just at random  or by chance  and i think these two plots um  are perhaps the most uh clear and um  the most relevant so we see that  individuals  crossing at the border are  clustered based on their degree of  ancestry  so individuals with  high indigenous ancestry tend to be in  the  central part of the region along the  border  whereas european are on the periphery  and if you think back to that previous  slide with the circles  southern mexico was in the central  area of the border which of course  based on my other slide that showed that  gradation and ancestry  estimates southern mexico and high  indigenous ancestry  should be the pattern that we would like  to see and that's what's actually played  out in the maps  so can we actually take this information  then and build this into a model  that can be used for prediction of that  geographic region in mexico  so i attempted first logistic regression  it explained only 38  of the variation and misclassified 43 of  the cases  significant improvement was achieved  with  machine learning methods so a neural  network using 12 nodes two hidden layers  activated by the hyperbolic tangent  function  with that type of model we got a really  high prediction accuracy  with even with level note cross  validation if we use a holdout sample  however  validation as validation on the r-square  and prediction accuracy was only  slightly lower and still pretty  remarkable  what i think i find most exciting is the  use of discriminate function  analysis because it is well known among  anthropologists  and it's very simply implemented and it  doesn't have the same black boxing  qualities that say a neural network does  and i found that discriminate analysis  produced  really exciting results overall  misclassification  is low at less than 12 percent if we  attempt to classify the individuals into  their region  of mexico and the best  classification rate is for the north and  i will say that the classification rate  is best for the north because  most likely because i had also more  northern samples  if once i balanced out the samples  north and south were quite equivalent  so what is the advantages of going  through this more  complex um approach to  addressing ancestry and questions of  biogeographic profile estimation rather  than just traditionally  estimating ancestry and providing that  within a case report  well using this biogeographic profile  approach  it's possible to identify the hot spots  where  we may expect to find remains and  mobilize workers to search those areas  and target areas where death occurs more  frequently  um and these would be on the pathways  that are now underserved or no longer  served by support systems  so there is a advantage for actually  recovering remains  it also provides valuable lines of  evidence to direct case investigations  or to support partial identifications if  our sending region  isn't known and family members from  migrant communities are not  reached are too fearful to report  they're missing  and cannot provide comparative dna  samples  the identification potential drops  significantly for the future  so we need a way that if we don't have  family members  coming to us to report or going having  the opportunity to go some  to someone um an ngo a humanitarian aid  group in their  location to report a missing family  member  how can we transmit that information  back  we need to know where to send it to  the method that i've developed here at  least  provides us with the first step towards  accessing that  origin information or sending region for  the individual  using relatively little information  that wouldn't already be collected in a  traditional forensic anthropological  case analysis gps coordinates  are obtained craniometric measurements  are taken routinely as part of the  standard ancestry estimate  we always know when the case was  recovered  and we have information on sex and time  should those two variables actually  prove to be  more informative i should go back and  say that  those variables didn't add much to the  model so it was simpler to remove them  without without complicating the model  so i think we have great potential for  thinking outside of the traditional  methods but that bring  in aspects of those traditional methods  the cranial metrics gis mapping is not  unfamiliar to forensic anthropologists  and to just integrate them in a  different way that allows us to answer  some of the most pressing questions  that are creating the greatest  impediments to  positive identification  and with that i would just like to thank  dr bruce anderson  at pima county um bruce is i'm sure to  the forensic community well known  and has been a pioneer and a tireless  advocate for identifying missing  migrants  and rebecca vanderwaal who is um the  student who i collaborated with  on uci uiuc um  and i'd like to thank um labyrinths  again for the opportunity to speak with  you today
 

